1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:02,790
Tesla made a car drive upside down.

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:05,703
Then it left the ramp,
rolled over in midair,

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:08,490
and landed.

4
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:10,910
Those are two different
engineering tricks,

5
00:00:10,920 --> 00:00:13,830
and the first one starts
with the air around you.

6
00:00:13,850 --> 00:00:17,749
On an earlier run, the car
stopped up here and just hung.

7
00:00:17,759 --> 00:00:20,985
Gravity was still pulling it
down with all of its weight.

8
00:00:20,995 --> 00:00:22,454
So what held it up?

9
00:00:22,464 --> 00:00:23,119
Air.

10
00:00:23,129 --> 00:00:24,070
At sea level,

11
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:28,950
air presses on everything with about ten
tons on every square meter from every

12
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:31,508
side, so normally, it cancels out.

13
00:00:31,518 --> 00:00:34,185
Tesla says fans pull
air from under the car,

14
00:00:34,195 --> 00:00:36,304
out of the thin gap against the ramp.

15
00:00:36,314 --> 00:00:37,484
Not all of it,

16
00:00:37,494 --> 00:00:40,997
just enough that the air left in
the gap pushes back more weakly.

17
00:00:41,007 --> 00:00:44,586
So the outside air wins and
presses the car into the ramp.

18
00:00:44,596 --> 00:00:45,881
The gap isn't pulling.

19
00:00:45,891 --> 00:00:48,040
The air around it is pushing.

20
00:00:48,050 --> 00:00:52,604
And that push always points into the
surface, on the floor, up the wall,

21
00:00:52,614 --> 00:00:54,140
even across the ceiling.

22
00:00:54,150 --> 00:00:57,386
But only if it beats the
car's weight, plus every bump,

23
00:00:57,396 --> 00:00:59,440
and only while the seal holds.

24
00:00:59,450 --> 00:01:02,257
Tesla says the fans
can pull up to two g's.

25
00:01:02,267 --> 00:01:05,709
Taken at face value, that's
about twice the car's weight.

26
00:01:05,750 --> 00:01:08,865
Race teams tried fan
cars back in the 1970s.

27
00:01:08,875 --> 00:01:11,685
They gripped so well
that rivals cried foul,

28
00:01:11,695 --> 00:01:14,385
and the cars were quickly
pushed out of racing.

29
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:17,026
Now watch what happens
when it leaves the ramp.

30
00:01:17,036 --> 00:01:21,140
The gap opens, air rushes back in,
and the grip is gone.

31
00:01:21,150 --> 00:01:23,597
In the air, the car needs a second trick.

32
00:01:23,607 --> 00:01:26,112
Tesla calls them cold-gas thrusters.

33
00:01:26,122 --> 00:01:28,894
Compressed air built
with help from SpaceX.

34
00:01:28,904 --> 00:01:31,094
No fuel, no flame.

35
00:01:31,350 --> 00:01:34,678
Throw something heavy one way,
and you get pushed the other way.

36
00:01:34,688 --> 00:01:38,734
Throw it from off to one side,
and you don't just move, you turn.

37
00:01:38,750 --> 00:01:40,877
The car does the same thing with gas.

38
00:01:40,887 --> 00:01:44,267
Gas goes out one way, and the
car gets pushed the opposite way.

39
00:01:44,277 --> 00:01:48,067
Push one side up and the other
side down, and the car rolls.

40
00:01:48,077 --> 00:01:50,372
Fire the opposite pair to stop the roll.

41
00:01:50,382 --> 00:01:54,440
Fire the gas downward, and the car
is pushed up, slowing its fall.

42
00:01:54,450 --> 00:01:56,751
Here's the real thing at half speed.

43
00:01:56,761 --> 00:02:00,317
A pair fires in opposite directions,
and the car turns over.

44
00:02:00,327 --> 00:02:03,817
Then the gas fires down to
ease it toward the ground.

45
00:02:04,150 --> 00:02:06,060
Then the showpiece.

46
00:02:06,070 --> 00:02:07,660
No ramp in sight.

47
00:02:07,850 --> 00:02:11,150
Over a row of Teslas, Semi included.

48
00:02:11,950 --> 00:02:14,140
The thrusters aren't just for flying.

49
00:02:14,150 --> 00:02:17,892
Tesla says the ones at the back also
shove the car forward at launch,

50
00:02:17,902 --> 00:02:20,240
harder than the tires alone could grip.

51
00:02:20,250 --> 00:02:23,640
The stream first showed a
recorded run that took one second.

52
00:02:23,650 --> 00:02:28,794
Then came a live run, which Tesla
says hit 60 in 0.81 seconds.

53
00:02:28,804 --> 00:02:31,074
Nobody timed that one independently.

54
00:02:31,450 --> 00:02:33,610
So which Roadster does what?

55
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:35,605
Tesla lists three versions.

56
00:02:35,615 --> 00:02:38,695
Performance has the fan
suction and no thrusters.

57
00:02:38,705 --> 00:02:40,793
Thrust adds thrusters at the back.

58
00:02:40,803 --> 00:02:42,613
Rocket gets thrusters all around,

59
00:02:42,623 --> 00:02:45,311
and it's the only version
Tesla lists with flight.

60
00:02:45,321 --> 00:02:47,348
It seats one, it's invite-only,

61
00:02:47,358 --> 00:02:50,970
and Tesla's own page says it isn't
approved for public road use.

62
00:02:50,980 --> 00:02:53,530
Production is targeted for 2028.

63
00:02:53,550 --> 00:02:56,224
Some things the demo didn't answer.

64
00:02:56,234 --> 00:02:57,666
How long can it stay up?

65
00:02:57,676 --> 00:03:01,709
How much air does each hop use,
and how fast do the tanks refill?

66
00:03:01,719 --> 00:03:03,873
And no one was driving from inside.

67
00:03:03,883 --> 00:03:06,693
Musk said the car ran itself for the demo.

68
00:03:07,150 --> 00:03:10,141
So how does one car pull off both tricks?

69
00:03:10,151 --> 00:03:12,031
Air pressure holds it to the ramp.

70
00:03:12,041 --> 00:03:14,098
Gas thrust steers it through the air.

71
00:03:14,108 --> 00:03:17,878
And a flying car you can drive
every day on public roads?

72
00:03:17,888 --> 00:03:19,798
This demo didn't show that.
